      "pins": [
        "J1.1",
        "J8.1",
        "D3.1",
        "Q2.2"
      ],
      "nets": [
        "NET0041"
      ],
      "observed": "The positive pin of the BAT connector (J1 pin 1) and the positive pin of the 5V_POWER connector (J8 pin 1) are drawn into the same node/net NET0041. That same node also feeds the anode of D3 (SS54) and the source side of the battery P-FET Q2 (IRLML6402).",
      "expected": "The specification defines two separate supply paths: the battery input is gated through the P-FET to the /BAT bus net, while the external 5V input is isolated from the board +5V rail by the SS54 series diode. The external 5V jack and the battery connector should not be merged on the same net.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "source": "schematic",
          "quote": "J8 5V_POWER pin 1 and J1 BAT pin 1 are connected together to the same green node that also connects to D3 SS54 and Q2 IRLML6402; net label NET0041 appears on that node."
        },
        {
          "source": "spec",
          "quote": "J5 | Conn_01x02, labelled BAT | Battery input connector, gated through Q1 to the /BAT bus net."
        },
        {
          "source": "spec",
          "quote": "J7 (labelled 5V_POWER) feeds the board's +5V rail through D1 (SS54 Schottky), which blocks current from flowing back out of the external-power jack."
        }
      ],
      "impact": "External 5V can appear on the battery node and, when the optional power switch turns Q2 on, be applied to the M5Stack BAT pin. Conversely, battery voltage can back-feed into the external 5V jack. This creates an unintended power path and can damage the M5Stack Core or the external supply.",
      "confidence": 0.72
    }
  ],
  "summary": "The schematic appears to merge the battery input and external 5V input on NET0041, contrary to the specification's two separate, diode-isolated power paths."
}

